I'm afraid this ascension report will be a little vague. After a
couple of Spork games I happened to notice I had a leftover game on
chiark which had been there for some months. Therefore I have no real
idea of how the character got through the very early game.

I've ascended every class in vanilla, but was gently unsatisfied with
my 3.3.1 archaeologist, who found a WoW on DL 2 and therefore had an
easy run of it. I guess I started this game as an attempt to plug that
gap.

I seem to have started by finding a coaligned altar on DL 4, the Mines
entrance level, and getting Snickersnee. I then cleaned out the Mines
with the combat power that gave me (ditching my pet after cleaning out
the Mines shops), and got Fire Brand after that; Fire Brand would be
my only source of fire resistance until the dragon corpses at the
Castle, which left me not using Excalibur until the late game except
against level drainers.

The entrance to Sokoban was the Big Room on DL 10; I had already
identified the amulet of reflection by zapping myself, so of course I
got the AoR Sokoban, but the other stuff was not unwelcome. Fort
Ludious turned up on DL 11 - I cleaned out the outside area, but that
didn't yield a DSM; I would make a blue DSM later in the main
dungeon. It still gave me a fair amount of useful stuff, enough to
then attempt the inside area and clear it.

I pressed on down to find the Quest on DL 16 along with a hostile
temple and another altar. I was Quest-qualified but didn't fancy the
Minion of Huhetotl without magic resistance, but I did clean out the
Quest levels - carefully, given the risk of a trapdoor or similar
dropping me on the final level.

I chased up a longsword, made Excalibur, and analysed the large pile
of stuff I'd accumulated. Smoky potions were confusion, which didn't
do me any good until the final round of stuff analysis immediately
before killing Rodney, but one unIDed spellbook proved to be identify,
which I had at 0% failure thanks to practicing divination spells with
light. Practicing healing spells with cure blindness also got cure
sickness at 0%, although I turned out not to need it except for
Jubilex.

I pressed down the main dungeon with my tinning kit (since I was
seeing giants); thrones on 20 and 23 gave me nothing, but a throne
room on level 21 both contained a bag of holding and gave a throne
wish - gray dragon scale mail. At that point I was pretty sure I had
it made if I could avoid stupid mistakes.

Medusa on 24; I got mind flayed. Castle on 25; I got mind flayed
again.  Less of a nuisance with identify at 0% failure, but still a
pain; the Castle also had two rust monsters and a disenchanter. Of
course, I came in the back and checked out the armour room (nothing)
first - getting fire and disintegration resistance off the dragons.
The wishes went on charging, speed boots, a shield of reflection, a
helm of telepathy, a cloak of displacement, and - er - something. A
magic marker?

Then I realised I hadn't done the Quest after getting my DSM. Ooops.
The Minion was not difficult, and then I realised the Orb of Detection
gave telepathy anyway; generally my wish/AK strategy this game was a
bit undirected.

I cleared out Gehennom - using some WoDeath charges on MMFs, which
would cost me later - straight down, with the spell of magic mapping
available. The demon lords were not a serious problem; I deliberately
approached Orcus from below, and got two wand charges for my pains; he
also added Yeenoghu to my bag. Arch-liches made a bit of a nuisance of
themselves.

With everything clear except Rodney himself, I returned to my stash (I
had controlled level teleport), did the final stuff analysis and
alchemy run - smoky potions yielded a wish for genocide, which got L
and h off my back at last. I walked (to conserve my supply of scrolls
of teleportation and restore lost level maps) up to DL 1 to drop off a
care package - K-rats, extra holy water, scrolls of gold detection,
etc. - walked back down to the stash, picked up a bunch more loot, and
zoomed off to kill Rodney. I ditched my pick-axe - good for weight
capacity, but led to its own problem below - because I had a million
wands of digging. Bagging it would have been best.

All my three wand of death charges whiffed when I tackled him in his
tower, which was annoying, but I had good luck with the Rodney rolls
so he never got to teleport away with anything. A new technique for me
is to pick up any cockatrice corpse I generate (with a careful shuffle
not to stash it into a less-fresh one) - then if Rodney turns up, I
rubber chicken him. This took care of his one random appearance in
Gehennom.

I used the stylish entrance to the Sanctum - missed the first time,
but I had sufficient SoTele in reserve to bounce out and in again,
landing near the inner chamber. I had a WoSDD, so bashed one bone
devil out the way, killed the High Priest, and drank the cursed PoGL.

The mysterious force was not too feisty, and I had no serious problems
on the way up - since gremlins were multiplying like crazy on Medusa,
I was very glad not to repeat it. I forgot to drop in at my stash on
the way up, but my care package on DL 1 had most of what I needed.

Unfortunately, the rubber chicken trick is no good if Rodney steals
the Amulet on Earth and you ditched your pickaxe. Fortunately, I
realised this just before getting myself stuck; my spell of Force Bolt
had long since expired. I killed him conventionally, but not before he
summoned a pile of minotaurs and generally made a nuisance of
himself.

After that Earth was easy with a nearby portal; Air was OK until an
Archon summoned a huge pile of air elementals and I was engulfed four
times in rapid succession, but I only had to use one PoFH. Rodney
turned up on Fire, but since the portal was very close I just ran for
it and killed him on Water; on Water I burned SoGD in quick succession
to get a fix on the portal, but why else bring them?

The press on Astral was tight, with many player monsters in play - I
saw Frosty and Giantslayer, but no Vorpy. I struggled into Famine
first, avoiding him - that was wrong, and he nibbled me on the way
out, but who cares? I completely avoided Death on the way in to her
altar, and as luck would have it that was the right one. I felt the
lack of confused taming, and the friendly Angel died.

